1
00:00:00,066 --> 00:00:08,469
This is the Smart Guided Troubleshooting Engine, team Nexora's prototype for Theme 2 of the Samsung PRISM Generative AI Hackathon.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:23,301
It takes a TechCorp Nexa complaint, together with the support article retrieved for it, and returns a validated, ordered troubleshooting plan. Every step comes from that article, and every Settings fix is one tap away.

3
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:27,957
People describe symptoms, but Settings speak in menus.

4
00:00:28,333 --> 00:00:39,960
The official kit has 20 complaints. Each one arrives with its SIIS article, as a title and markdown content, and the answer must match the official sample output.

5
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:50,795
Our targets: under three hundred milliseconds for known issues, under eight seconds for new ones, zero invented steps, and zero invalid deeplinks.

6
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:56,643
Language models reason, retrieval grounds, and deterministic code validates.

7
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:08,992
The article sent with the complaint is the only source of steps. We split it into sections, keep the blocks that answer the complaint, and turn TechCorp's phrasing into canonical steps.

8
00:01:09,333 --> 00:01:19,900
Steps are ordered from safe settings to critical actions, each Settings navigation gets its exact deeplink, and eleven validators check the plan before it is cached.

9
00:01:20,333 --> 00:01:35,770
Here is the demo. It runs on a laptop CPU, with free, local models, and an empty cache. The picker sends each official complaint together with its SIIS article, exactly like the API contract.

10
00:01:36,133 --> 00:01:41,271
Row twenty-one: the screen inputs are delayed, and the touch is laggy.

11
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:55,702
This is a new request, so the full pipeline ran, in 1.9 seconds. The article is a numbered touchscreen procedure, so it is kept whole: 11 actions, all copied from the article.

12
00:01:56,066 --> 00:02:03,659
Automatic settings come first. Turn on touch sensitivity carries the catalog deeplink for the Display screen.

13
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,698
Manual checks come next, and the disruptive steps, safe mode and a factory data reset, come last.

14
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,453
Now the same complaint, with the same article, again.

15
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:28,552
This time the validated plan comes from the cache, in 34 milliseconds, with zero model calls. A plan built from a different article is never served.

16
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:37,556
And the response is pure JSON. With view equals contract, it has exactly the shape of the official sample output.

17
00:02:38,866 --> 00:02:44,980
Row eight is harder: the main screen stays small, sent with a long screen mirroring guide.

18
00:02:45,333 --> 00:02:52,736
Only one tip in that guide answers the complaint, changing the aspect ratio, so the plan has one action.

19
00:02:53,066 --> 00:03:02,904
The evidence trail shows why: 1 of 20 instruction blocks were kept, with the relevance score of every block, and the source sentence behind every step.

20
00:03:03,266 --> 00:03:08,170
A support team can see exactly what was used, and what was left out.

21
00:03:09,466 --> 00:03:14,685
Row one, an email screen that goes blank, shows step groups.

22
00:03:15,066 --> 00:03:23,614
Clearing the cache and clearing the data are two separate navigations, so the action has two step groups, each with its own deeplink.

23
00:03:24,866 --> 00:03:31,779
Without an article, the engine retrieves one itself: the screen does not rotate when I turn the tablet.

24
00:03:32,133 --> 00:03:39,166
It grounded the plan in the screen rotation article, in 943 milliseconds on the CPU.

25
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:50,900
When nothing answers, it refuses to guess. My Nexa watch won't sync my steps returns an empty plan with a no match flag, and the query is logged for the content team.

26
00:03:51,333 --> 00:04:01,730
On all 20 official complaints, every output is schema valid and passes all eleven rule checks, and every one of the 308 steps is found in its article.

27
00:04:02,066 --> 00:04:12,409
Repeats return from the cache in 28 milliseconds at the ninety-fifth percentile, and new requests in about 1.5 seconds, on a laptop CPU.

28
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:25,195
On our synthetic regression kit, gold step accuracy stays at 3 out of 3, and 80 percent of unseen paraphrases are served without any model call. All 69 tests pass.

29
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:38,891
The main limitations: the official kit has no deeplink catalog and no gold answers, so our catalog is synthetic and accuracy is measured on synthetic gold, and relevance inside long articles is a heuristic.

30
00:04:39,266 --> 00:04:46,934
Everything is open source, starts with docker compose up, and costs nothing to run. Thank you.
